#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Fake ``claude-session`` for gatectl's tests. Test fixture only: nothing in src/ uses it.

Pinned to claude-session v0.45.2 (commit c42491578a5309421c1fa67afbdd9f9650fd615b): the
argument grammar, the JSON shapes (checked against ``tests/pinned/claude-session-0.45.2/``),
the exit codes and the error envelope mirror that version's documented interface. It runs no
model and no sandbox; every outcome is scripted by a scenario.

Implemented: ``start`` (new session only), ``exec --session-id``, ``status``, ``list``,
``stop`` and ``forget`` (single id). Any other command, or a flag outside the documented
grammar of these, exits 2 like an unknown flag. Python 3 standard library only.

Environment (all read on every call):

``FAKE_CS_STATE``            directory holding the fake's sessions (required).
``FAKE_CS_LOG``              file receiving one JSON line per call and one per exit (optional).
``FAKE_CS_SCENARIO``         scenario name (default ``valid``); ``<launch scenario>@exec`` makes a
                             launch failure happen at adoption instead of at ``start``.
``FAKE_CS_BEHAVIOR_<CMD>``   per-command override for START, EXEC, STATUS, LIST, STOP, FORGET:
                             ``fail[:<exit>]``, ``hang``, ``hang_ignore_term``, ``garbage``
                             (unparseable stdout), ``json_wrong`` (valid JSON of the wrong shape),
                             ``empty``, and for START ``hang_after_allocate``.
``FAKE_CS_NONCE``            nonce to echo (default: the 32-hex nonce found in the staged prompt).
``FAKE_CS_DECISION``, ``FAKE_CS_CONFIDENCE``, ``FAKE_CS_REASONS`` (JSON list)
                             content of the valid verdict artifact (allow, 0.95, ["fake reason"]).
``FAKE_CS_DELAY_EXEC``       seconds ``exec`` stays live before it ends (interruptible).
``FAKE_CS_HANG_MAX``         longest a hang lasts, in seconds (default 120).
``FAKE_CS_SANDBOX_ACTIVE``   ``0`` or ``1``: what ``status`` reports as ``sandbox_active``.
``FAKE_CS_EXIT0_OUTCOME``    outcome printed with exit 0 by the ``exit0_not_done`` scenario.
"""

